<title>UK: EU, US seen on collision course over grain trade.</title>
<headline>EU, US seen on collision course over grain trade.</headline>
<dateline>LONDON 1997-04-24</dateline>
<text>
<p>Europe and the United States are on a collision course over cereals export policy, according to Grain and Feed Trade Association president David Small.</p>
<p>&quot;Currently it does seem that the US and the EU are moving in diametrically opposed directions,&quot; Small said in a speech prepared for delivery at GAFTA's annual dinner on Thursday evening.</p>
<p>In its 1996 Agricultural Act, the US had decoupled agricultural payments from production, eliminated the annual set-aside programmes and allowed producers to respond to market forces, rather than to government policies, Small said.</p>
<p>It had refrained from using export subsidies on wheat exports. &quot;These policy changes are expected to result in greater supplies and increased exports of grain at world market prices,&quot; Small said.</p>
<p>&quot;In contrast, the EU has maintained its system of supply management and price control for grain production and trade, with a mandatory set-aside programme and strict market management controls on exports,&quot; Small said.</p>
<p>--Christopher Lyddon, London newsroom +44 171 542 7928</p>
